 StyleBox["PASS  ",
  FontWeight->Bold,
  FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0.5, 0]],
 "with the grammar as a constraint every answer is a valid prefix tree"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"16d20f21-c0dc-40f1-8a35-58aafe2f76f1"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["PASS  ",
  FontWeight->Bold,
  FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0.5, 0]],
 "'any in beam' is never below 'top answer' (it counts the top answer too)"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"541ab7d2-ceef-4b63-b1d8-de253b841ac7"],

Cell["\<\
What to try next: more training pairs or rounds (owSettings), a larger d, or \
a curriculum from short to long expressions. A single wrong token spoils a \
whole expansion, so longer targets are harder; the oracle shows exactly which \
answers are wrong.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"c61eb3e0-3bee-41fa-a844-fb1d7cc1fd47"],

Cell["Where this goes in TensorNets", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"5661df07-9963-4dfa-a596-0e12db6445cf"],

Cell["\<\
tnParserNet in ff_tensornets_new.nb is this same kind of \
encoder\[Dash]decoder, mapping tokens to the prefix tree; tnPolicyNet and \
tnValueNet reuse the encoder. The difference in the release is that \
simplification is done by the policy net choosing rewrite rules, which the \
exact engine applies, so every step is sound, rather than by writing the \
whole answer at once as here.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"38f82299-899d-4932-b550-fe776eb1d428"],

